/ref 17592186332764 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186064280 1038 #edb/ref 17592186332765 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186064280 1038 #edb/ref 17592186332766 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186064280 1038 #edb/ref 17592186332767 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186064280 1038 #edb/ref 17592186332768 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186064280 1038 #edb/ref 17592186332769 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186064287 1038 #edb/ref 17592186332847 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186064325 1038 #edb/ref 17592186332868 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186064326 1038 #edb/ref 17592186332706 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186064327 1038 #edb/ref 17592186332684 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186064327 1038 #edb/ref 17592186332703 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186064327 1038 #edb/ref 17592186332704 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186064327 1038 #edb/ref 17592186332705 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186064388 1038 #edb/ref 17592186332656 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186064852 1068 #edb/ref 17592186063139 13194139533424 17592186045585 false] [17592186064852 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332429 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186064852 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186064852 1071 "$\\int \\operatorname{tan}⁡x(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186064852 1071 "What is $\\displaystyle \\int\\tan x(\\sec x + 2\\cos x)dx$?" 13194139533424 17592186045585 false] [17592186064852 1074 "We multiply out the parentheses and then use the identity $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{tan}⁡x(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+2\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186064860 1068 #edb/ref 17592186063186 13194139533424 17592186045585 false] [17592186064860 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332465 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186064860 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186064860 1071 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{1/2}^{1}\\frac{1}{x^{6}}dx$." 13194139533424 17592186045585 false] [17592186064860 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{1/2}^{1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186064860 1074 "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}{x}^{-6}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-6+1}}{-6+1}{|}_{1/2}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-5}}{(-5)}{|}_{1/2}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5{x}^{5}}{|}_{1/2}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}(\\frac{1}{{1}^{5}}-\\frac{1}{{(1/2)}^{5}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}(1-{2}^{5}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}(1-32) \\\\  & =\\frac{31}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186064862 1068 #edb/ref 17592186063198 13194139533424 17592186045585 false] [17592186064862 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332452 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186064862 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186064862 1071 "$\\int \\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186064862 1071 "What is $\\displaystyle \\int\\cot xdx$?" 13194139533424 17592186045585 false] [17592186064862 1074 "We use the identity $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}$ and rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Then \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186064864 1068 #edb/ref 17592186063210 13194139533424 17592186045585 false] [17592186064864 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332595 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186064864 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186064864 1071 "$\\int \\frac{{cos}^{3}⁡x+{cot}^{2}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186064864 1071 "What is $\\displaystyle \\int\\frac{\\cos^{3} x + \\cot^{2} x}{\\cos^{2} x}dx$?" 13194139533424 17592186045585 false] [17592186064864 1074 "We can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{{cos}^{3}⁡x+{cot}^{2}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}({cos}^{3}⁡x+{cot}^{2}⁡x)\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe then use the fact that ${cot}^{2}⁡x=\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}$ to express this as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}({cos}^{3}⁡x+\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x}{{sin}^{2}⁡x})\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{cos}^{3}⁡x+{cot}^{2}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}({cos}^{3}⁡x+\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x}{{sin}^{2}⁡x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot {cos}^{3}⁡x+\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x}{{sin}^{2}⁡x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{csc}^{2}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186064865 1068 #edb/ref 17592186063216 13194139533424 17592186045585 false] [17592186064865 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332404 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186064865 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186064865 1071 "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int\\frac{\\tan^{2} x}{8\\sin^{2} x}dx$." 13194139533424 17592186045585 false] [17592186064865 1071 "Calculate $\\int \\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡x}{8{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186064865 1074 "We can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡x}{8{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{8}\\int {tan}^{2}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe then use the fact that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}$ and $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡x}{8{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{8}\\int {tan}^{2}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\int {(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\int \\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\int \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\int {(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})}^{2}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\int {sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186064867 1068 #edb/ref 17592186063228 13194139533424 17592186045585 false] [17592186064867 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332378 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186064867 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186064867 1071 "$\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4y-1}}\\,\\text{d}y=$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186064867 1071 "What is $\\displaystyle \\int\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4y - 1}}dy$?" 13194139533424 17592186045585 false] [17592186064867 1074 "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int (4y-1)^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nNow, we let $u=4y-1.$ Then differentiating with respect to $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}y}=4\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}y=\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $u$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (4y-1)^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int {u}^{-1/2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int {u}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{(1/2)}{u}^{1/2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{4}\\cdot {u}^{1/2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4y-1)^{1/2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{4y-1}}{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}[\\frac{\\sqrt{4y-1}}{2}+C] & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}[\\sqrt{4y-1}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}[(4y-1)^{1/2}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(4y-1)^{-1/2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(4y-1)+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(4y-1)^{-1/2}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4y-1}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186064874 1068 #edb/ref 17592186063270 13194139533424 17592186045585 false] [17592186064874 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332391 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186064874 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186064874 1071 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2}\\frac{5x - 1}{2x + 1}dx$." 13194139533424 17592186045585 false] [17592186064874 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{5x-1}{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186064874 1074 "First, we pull out the factor of $2$ from the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{5x-1}{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{5x-1}{2(x+\\frac{1}{2})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{5x-1}{(x+\\frac{1}{2})}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we draw up our synthetic division table to decompose the integrand into its quotient and remainder.\n\n|  | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $5$ | $-1$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-\\frac{5}{2}$ |\n|  | $5$ | $-\\frac{7}{2}$ |\n\nSo from the synthetic division, we get that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5x-1}{(x+\\frac{1}{2})} & =5-\\frac{(\\frac{7}{2})}{(x+\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =5-\\frac{7}{2(x+\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =5-\\frac{7}{2x+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{5x-1}{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(5-\\frac{7}{2x+1})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[5{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\text{d}x-7{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[5x{|}_{0}^{2}-\\frac{7}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|{|}_{0}^{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[5(2-0)-\\frac{7}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2\\cdot 2+1|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2\\cdot 0+1|)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[10-\\frac{7}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5] \\\\  & =5-\\frac{7}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186064879 1068 #edb/ref 17592186063300 13194139533424 17592186045585 false] [17592186064879 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332529 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186064879 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186064879 1071 "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int(\\frac{1}{2 + 4x} + \\frac{1}{2 - 4x})dx$." 13194139533424 17592186045585 false] [17592186064879 1071 "Calculate $\\int (\\frac{1}{2+4x}+\\frac{1}{2-4x})\\,\\text{d}x.$ Assume that $K$ is an arbitrary positive constant." 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186064879 1074 "Let's solve this using the general formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\frac{1}{2+4x}+\\frac{1}{2-4x})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{2+4x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{2-4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2+4x|-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2-4x|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{2+4x}{2-4x}|+\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡K \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|\\frac{2+4x}{2-4x}|)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the laws of logarithms to write the result as a single logarithm." 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186064880 1068 #edb/ref 17592186063306 13194139533424 17592186045585 false] [17592186064880 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332313 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186064880 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186064880 1071 "$\\int \\frac{6}{1+9{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186064880 1071 "What is $\\displaystyle \\int\\frac{6}{1 + 9x^{2}}dx$?" 13194139533424 17592186045585 false] [17592186064880 1074 "This integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $9{x}^{2}=(3x)^{2}.$\n\nSo. let's substitute $u=3x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{6}{1+9{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =6\\int \\frac{1}{1+(3x)^{2}}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(3x)+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186064881 1068 #edb/ref 17592186063312 13194139533424 17592186045585 false] [17592186064881 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332323 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186064881 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186064881 1071 "$\\int \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{49-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186064881 1071 "What is $\\displaystyle \\int\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{49 - x^{2}}}dx$?" 13194139533424 17592186045585 false] [17592186064881 1074 "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{49-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{49\\cdot (1-{x}^{2}/49)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{7}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}/49}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{7}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x/7)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{7})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{7}.$ Differentiating, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{7}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=7\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{49-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{3}{7}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x/7)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{7}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot (7\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =3\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}} \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x}{7})+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186064882 1068 #edb/ref 17592186063318 13194139533424 17592186045585 false] [17592186064882 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332628 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186064882 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186064882 1071 "$\\int \\frac{1}{9+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186064882 1071 "What is $\\displaystyle \\int\\frac{1}{9 + x^{2}}dx$?" 13194139533424 17592186045585 false] [17592186064882 1074 "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{9+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{9\\cdot (1+{x}^{2}/9)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}/9}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{1+(x/3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{3})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{3}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{3}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=3\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{9+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{1+(x/3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\cdot (3\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{1+{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186064883 1068 #edb/ref 17592186063324 13194139533424 17592186045585 false] [17592186064883 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332493 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186064883 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186064883 1071 "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π/2}x\\cos (x)dx$." 13194139533424 17592186045585 false] [17592186064883 1071 "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186064883 1074 "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x & ⇒ & {u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & ⇒ & v=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2}-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-0\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)]+[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)] \\\\  & =[\\frac{\\pi }{2}-0]+[0-1] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186064884 1068 #edb/ref 17592186063330 13194139533424 17592186045585 false] [17592186064884 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332347 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186064884 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186064884 1071 "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1/3}2xe^{3x}dx$." 13194139533424 17592186045585 false] [17592186064884 1071 "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{1/3}2x{e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186064884 1074 "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=2x & ⇒ & {u}^{′}=2 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{3x} & ⇒ & v=\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 2x\\cdot {e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =2x\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{3x}}{3}-\\int (\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{3})\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1/3}2x{e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\frac{2}{3}x{e}^{3x}|}_{0}^{1/3}-\\frac{2}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1/3}{e}^{3x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{2}{3}x{e}^{3x}|}_{0}^{1/3}-{\\frac{2}{9}{e}^{3x}|}_{0}^{1/3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}[\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3(1/3)}-0\\cdot {e}^{3\\cdot 0}]-\\frac{2}{9}[{e}^{3(1/3)}-{e}^{3\\cdot 0}] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}[\\frac{e}{3}-0]-\\frac{2}{9}[e-1] \\\\  & =\\frac{2e}{9}-\\frac{2e}{9}+\\frac{2}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186064898 1068 #edb/ref 17592186063414 13194139533424 17592186045585 false] [17592186064898 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332324 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186064898 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186064898 1071 "![](images/2d/2dbcf9be5f2a9ffe89bb0d8601e5be931619bbda9647ce5cf285365085183e74.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y = f(x)$, defined on $[- 2, 3]$. Find the intervals on which the function $f(x)$ is concave up." 13194139533424 17592186045585 false] [17592186064898 1071 "![](images/2d/2dbcf9be5f2a9ffe89bb0d8601e5be931619bbda9647ce5cf285365085183e74.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=f(x),$ defined on $[-2,3].$ Find the intervals on which the function $f(x)$ is concave up." 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186064898 1074 "The concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($⌣$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($⌢$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\".\n\nWe can see that the function is curved upward like a smile ($⌣$) over the interval $(-1,1).$\n\n![](images/1c/1c5e36abaafab3c3984edd11