17592186331282 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332655 1075 #edb/ref 17592186331734 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332655 1075 #edb/ref 17592186331752 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332655 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332655 1077 "selection" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332655 1079 #edb/ref 17592186331734 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332655 1080 #uuid "ffe1e563-5920-519e-afea-2f14d673c7ca" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332656 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332360 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332656 1069 "q-49008" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332656 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332656 1071 "Ivan and Elisabeth have a date at the mall.\nIvan arrived at the agreed time, but Elisabeth is late. After $30$ minutes of waiting, Ivan decides to go home.\n\nIvan travels north from the mall with a speed of $4\\text{km/h}.$ Elisabeth travels west toward the mall. When Ivan is $1.2\\,\\text{km}$ from the mall, Elisabeth is $0.5\\,\\text{km}$ from the mall. If the distance between them increases at a speed of $1.5\\,\\text{km/h},$ what is Elisabeth's speed?" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332656 1072 #uuid "2b4fe11c-dbab-5ed9-be43-8981f9bd07de" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332656 1074 "We start by drawing a diagram of the problem.\n\n![](images/2d/2ddd52ba5c32fd8fa9b1a24ec9770bfda5103f61d365c49c13c1f52fbaa799bd.png)\n\nIn the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the distance between Elisabeth and the mall,\n\n- $y$ is the distance between Ivan and the mall,\n\n- $z$ is the distance between Ivan and Elisabeth.\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $x,$ which is $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ \n\nWe know that $x=0.5\\,\\text{km}$ and $y=1.2\\,\\text{km}.$ We can find $z$ using the Pythagorean Theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {0.5}^{2}+{1.2}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {z}^{2} & =1.69 \\\\ z & =1.3\\,\\text{m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that:\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=4\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h},$\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}=1.5\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h}.$\n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute in the known values, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(0.5)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+2(1.2)(4) & =2(1.3)(1.5) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =3.9-9.6 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-5.7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Elisabeth is approaching the mall with a speed of $5.7\\,\\text{km/h}.$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332657 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332488 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332657 1069 "q-49024" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332657 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332657 1071 "For which value of $x$ does the function $f(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡x}{x}$ defined for $x\\in (0,\\infty )$ have a local maximum?" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332657 1072 #uuid "56c5438a-da5f-5788-885e-7f027b595c02" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332657 1074 "First, we note that \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡x}{x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}{{x}^{2}{ln}^{2}⁡(10)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ on $(0,\\infty )$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)} & =0 \\\\ 1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =1 \\\\ x & =e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=e$ is a stationary point. Next, to determine whether this point is a local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=1,{e}^{2}$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)}{(1)^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)}=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)}>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}({e}^{2}) & =\\frac{1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2})}{(e)^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)}=-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(10)}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(0,e)$ | $e$ | $(e,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $1$ |  | ${e}^{2}$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical point.\n\n- At $x=e,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=e$ is a local maximum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has a local maximum at $x=e.$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332658 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332246 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332658 1069 "q-49025" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332658 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332658 1071 "Given the function $f(x)=(x-1)^{3}{e}^{x}$ for $x\\in [0,\\infty ),$ which of the following statements are true?" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332658 1072 #uuid "5efe5f3d-27dd-5209-97cf-8075220fea7c" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332658 1074 "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =(x-1)^{3}{e}^{x}+3(x-1)^{2}{e}^{x} \\\\  & =(x-1)^{2}{e}^{x}(x-1+3) \\\\  & =(x-1)^{2}(x+2){e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n(x-1)^{2}(x+2){e}^{x}=0\\,⟹\\,x=-2,\\,x=1.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that ${e}^{x}>0$ for all values of $x.$\n\nSo, $x=1$ is the only stationary point of $f(x)$ on $[0,\\infty ).$ Next, to determine whether this point is a local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=0,2$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(0) & =(0-1)^{2}(0+2){e}^{0}=2>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =(2-1)^{2}(2+2){e}^{2}=4{e}^{2}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $[0,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $0$ |  | $2$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ |  | $↗$ |\n\nThe first derivative does not change sign at $x=1.$ Therefore, we conclude that $x=1$ is neither a minimum nor a maximum.\n\nTherefore, the first derivative test shows that the function has no local extrema on $[0,\\infty ).$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332659 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332597 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332659 1069 "q-49026" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332659 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332659 1071 "Given the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{e}^{x}},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=0$ is a local maximum\n- $x=0$ is neither a local minimum nor a local maximum\n- $x=3$ is a local minimum" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332659 1072 #uuid "31bc62dc-b78f-5686-a2a7-468fc236acf0" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332659 1074 "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{3{x}^{2}{e}^{x}-{x}^{3}{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}}=\\frac{3{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}}{{e}^{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}}{{e}^{x}} & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(3-x) & =0 \\\\ x=0,3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=0,\\,3.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,1,4$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =\\frac{3(-1)^{2}-(-1)^{3}}{{e}^{-1}}=4e>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{3(1)^{2}-(1)^{3}}{{e}^{1}}=\\frac{2}{e}>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(4) & =\\frac{3(4)^{2}-(4)^{3}}{{e}^{4}}=-\\frac{16}{{e}^{4}}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,3)$ | $3$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $1$ |  | $4$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ |  | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=0,$ the first derivative does not change the sign. Therefore, $x=0$ is neither a local minimum nor a local maximum.\n\n- At $x=3,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=3$ is a local maximum.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332660 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332469 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332660 1069 "q-49033" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332660 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332660 1071 "For which value of $x$ does the function $f(x)=(1-x){e}^{x}$ have a local maximum?" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332660 1072 #uuid "75b6e158-a189-5287-a3eb-b3b3f9bedf07" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332660 1074 "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=(1-x)\\cdot {e}^{x}-{e}^{x}=-x{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nSolving for ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x{e}^{x} & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=0$ is a stationary point. Next, to determine whether this point is a local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,1$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =-(-1){e}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{e}>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =-(1){e}^{1}=-e<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $1$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical point.\n\n- At $x=0,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=0$ is a local maximum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has a local maximum at $x=0.$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332661 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332632 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332661 1069 "q-49035" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332661 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332661 1071 "Given the function $f(x)={x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+12x-5,$ which of the following statements are true?" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332661 1072 #uuid "7ba9732b-d7d0-57c6-ab80-6fd54deb292c" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332661 1074 "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}-12x+12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-12x+12 & =0 \\\\ 3({x}^{2}-4x+4) & =0 \\\\ 3(x-2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x=2$ is the only stationary point of $f(x).$ Next, to determine whether this point is a local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=0,3$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(0) & =3(0)^{2}-12(0)+12=12>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(3) & =3(3)^{2}-12(3)+12=3>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $0$ |  | $3$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ |  | $↗$ |\n\nThe first derivative does not change sign at $x=2.$ Therefore, we conclude that $x=2$ is neither a minimum nor a maximum.\n\nHence, the first derivative test shows that the function has no local extrema." 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332662 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332346 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332662 1069 "q-49049" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332662 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332662 1071 "![](images/c9/c924791712d00c951d9718fa976a00f35b2b3deb35d83301431ddb55d22326de.png)\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ on the curve $y={e}^{2x}$ that is closest to the origin (as shown above) satisfies the equation \n\n$$\n2x+=0.\n$$\n\n \nWhat is the missing expression?" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332662 1072 #uuid "f8a959fd-ee39-5972-83df-6d4193cfe274" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332662 1074 "Let's consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(0,0)$ and a general point $P(x,y):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-0)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y={e}^{2x}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+({e}^{2x})^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{e}^{4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x+4{e}^{4x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332663 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332330 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332663 1069 "q-49051" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332663 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332663 1071 "![](images/22/22505181ad0a10e74a158edabc949444055f1deda57286ff63674c32f2dc79c7.png)\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ on the curve $y={log}_{2}⁡x$  that is closest to the origin (as shown above) satisfies the equation \n\n$$\n2x+=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nWhat is the missing expression?" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332663 1072 #uuid "a0b5476f-0e78-5366-afe4-a0704f4f9240" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332663 1074 "Let's consider the *squared* distance $S$ between $(0,0)$ and a general point $P(x,y):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-0)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe require that the point $P$ lies on the curve $y={log}_{2}⁡x.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+({log}_{2}⁡x)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{log}_{2}^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum distance, we take the first derivative of $S(x)$ and equate it to zero. This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x)=2x+2{log}_{2}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} & =0 \\\\ 2x+\\frac{2{log}_{2}⁡x}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332664 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332327 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332664 1069 "q-49067" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332664 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332664 1071 "What is the antiderivative of ${(1+\\frac{1}{x})}^{2}?$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332664 1072 #uuid "edf6e555-e835-5bc6-9e0b-944e25006b84" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332664 1074 "Expanding the binomial and applying the sum and constant factor rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {(1+\\frac{1}{x})}^{2}\\text{d}x & =\\int (1+\\frac{2}{x}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\text{d}x+2\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x+2ln⁡|x|+\\frac{{x}^{-2+1}}{-2+1}+C \\\\  & =x+2ln⁡|x|-\\frac{1}{x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, let's rewrite the constant so that we can combine it with the natural logarithm. For any constant $C,$ we can always write $C=2ln⁡K$ where $K$ is some other positive constant. Doing this allows us to combine the constant with $2ln⁡|x|$ term, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {(1+\\frac{1}{x})}^{2}\\text{d}x & =x-\\frac{1}{x}+2ln⁡|x|+2ln⁡K \\\\  & =x-\\frac{1}{x}+2(ln⁡|x|+ln⁡K) \\\\  & =x-\\frac{1}{x}+2ln⁡(K|x|).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332665 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332555 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332665 1069 "q-49075" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332665 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332665 1071 "If ${f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+1}-4{x}^{3},$ then $f(x)=$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332665 1072 #uuid "58be8fc4-bf7c-5658-9dd6-29f09252f095" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332665 1074 "Recall that \n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x)=\\int {f}^{′}(x)\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+1}-4{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int 4{x}^{3}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x-4\\int {x}^{3}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x-4\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+C \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x-{x}^{4}+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332666 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332216 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332666 1069 "q-49100" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332666 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332666 1071 "Find the integral of ${(2-\\frac{1}{x})}^{2}$ with respect to $x.$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332666 1072 #uuid "3c37bd55-c0e8-559f-80d9-e5fdbb837ece" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332666 1074 "Expanding the binomial and applying the sum and constant factor rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {(2-\\frac{1}{x})}^{2}\\text{d}x & =\\int (4-\\frac{4}{x}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int 4\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{4}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\int \\text{d}x-4\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4x-4ln⁡|x|+\\frac{{x}^{-2+1}}{-2+1}+C \\\\  & =4x-4ln⁡|x|-\\frac{1}{x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332667 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332485 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332667 1069 "q-49121" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332667 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332667 1071 "Calculate $\\int csc⁡x(3cot⁡x+2csc⁡x)\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332667 1072 #uuid "b36d025f-bace-58ef-adb7-3885184b511c" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332667 1074 "Recall that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {csc}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =-cot⁡x+C, \\\\ \\int csc⁡xcot⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =-csc⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int csc⁡x(3cot⁡x+2csc⁡x)\\text{d}x & =\\int (3csc⁡xcot⁡x+2{csc}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int 3csc⁡xcot⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+\\int 2{csc}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int csc⁡xcot⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+2\\int {csc}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3(-csc⁡x)+2(-cot⁡x)+C \\\\  & =-3csc⁡x-2cot⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332668 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332638 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332668 1069 "q-49122" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332668 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332668 1071 "Calculate $\\int sec⁡x(7sec⁡x+4tan⁡x)\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332668 1072 #uuid "c3a5d5fb-8f22-572d-9122-04a2c3a1b6ad" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332668 1074 "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int sec⁡xtan⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =sec⁡x+C, \\\\ \\int {sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =tan⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int sec⁡x(7sec⁡x+4tan⁡x)\\text{d}x & =\\int (7{sec}^{2}⁡x+4sec⁡xtan⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int 7{sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+\\int 4sec⁡xtan⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =7\\int {sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+4\\int sec⁡xtan⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =7tan⁡x+4sec⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332669 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332287 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332669 1069 "q-49129" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332669 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332669 1071 "$\\int (\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}-6{x}^{2})\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332669 1072 #uuid "1473ed23-4c35-5374-b722-df11f2918a3f" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332669 1074 "Recall that \n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x+C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}-6{x}^{2})\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int 6{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x-6\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x-6\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+C \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x-2{x}^{3}+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332670 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332276 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332670 1069 "q-49131" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332670 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332670 1071 "$\\int (\\frac{4}{1+{x}^{2}}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332670 1072 #uuid "7235061c-31e7-5f21-9e2a-41d26e24ad09" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332670 1074 "Recall that \n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\frac{4}{1+{x}^{2}}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{4}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\int \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x-(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+C \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332671 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332587 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332671 1069 "q-49133" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332671 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332671 1071 "$\\int (\\frac{6}{1+{x}^{2}}-9)\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332671 1072 #uuid "7f0fd041-ce90-54f7-824e-5b0234fc7192" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332671 1074 "Recall that \n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\frac{6}{1+{x}^{2}}-9)\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{6}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int 9\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\int \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x-9\\int \\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x-9x+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332672 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332462 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332672 1069 "q-49136" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332672 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332672 1071 "If ${f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{2}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ then $f(x)=$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332672 1072 #uuid "93133dfc-bc5a-589b-9b21-0d9909f27fa0" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332672 1074 "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{arcsec}\\,x.\n$$\n\nThen we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x)=\\int {f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{2}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{2}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x-3\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\text{ arcsec }x-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332673 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332235 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332673 1069 "q-49150" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332673 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332673 1071 "$\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}-7}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332673 1072 #uuid "4c5875e3-9603-540e-a71c-3edccf17389a" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332673 1074 "This integral doesn't look like one of the basic ones. However, we can manipulate the numerator a bit  to make it look more like the denominator, which reveals a more familiar expression: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-7}{{x}^{2}+1} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+1-1-7}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+1)-8}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{{x}^{2}+1}}{\\cancel{{x}^{2}+1}}-\\frac{8}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{8}{{x}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemembering that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C,\n$$\n\nwe can now calculate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)=\\int {f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (1-\\frac{8}{{x}^{2}+1})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\text{d}x-\\int \\frac{8}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\text{d}x-8\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x-8\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332674 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332289 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332674 1069 "q-49168" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332674 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332674 1071 "![](images/96/96622ea1476cf79ec3ff6d6486d77eda465de80d005304f542b4aadfb6be2b7a.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of two line segments. Find the value of ${\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332674 1072 #uuid "2169d87d-726c-59ec-9148-d23e772ce2b7" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332674 1074 "The graph consists of two parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-3,0]$ and the line segment for $x\\in (0,4].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-3}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-3,0].$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =4.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (0,4].$ The corresponding region is a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =ab \\\\  & =2\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{2}=-8.\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-3}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4.5-8 \\\\  & =-3.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332675 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332245 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332675 1069 "q-49169" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332675 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332675 1071 "![](images/55/555b702c1903f8cc4e379ad4281aa86708757755e4f9fb14b0ec7790a80a17dd.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, find the value of \n${\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332675 1072 #uuid "bf1250a6-33da-5c83-b34e-994a1715d47c" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332675 1074 "The graph consists of $3$ parts: the arc of the quarter-circle for $x\\in [-4,-2),$ the arc of the quarter-circle for $x\\in (-2,0],$ and the line segment for $x\\in (0,4].$ So, we can use the additive property of integrals to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-4}^{-2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{-2}^{-2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{-2}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={{{\\int }_{-4}^{-2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-2}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-4,-2).$ The corresponding region is a quarter-circle with the radius of $2$, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\pi \\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (-2,0].$ The corresponding region is a quarter-circle with the radius of $2$, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\pi \\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (0,4].$  The corresponding region is t