d the line segment for $x\\in (0,3).$ So, we can use the additive property of integrals to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-4}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={{{\\int }_{-4}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-4,0].$ The corresponding region is a combination of a semi-circle of radius $2$ and a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\pi {r}^{2})+bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2})+4(2) \\\\  & =2\\pi +8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (0,3).$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+3)(3) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =(2\\pi +8)+6 \\\\  & =2\\pi +14.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332683 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332213 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332683 1069 "q-49215" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332683 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332683 1071 "![](images/11/11cf2a28b6ce6281de0299a4a585bc3c98bf891f6d958401f22aab2fc6ce5ef2.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, find the value of ${\\int }_{-4}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332683 1072 #uuid "9df603d0-bbe1-5a34-9865-d622dab84e14" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332683 1074 "The graph consists of three parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-4,-2],$ the arc of a semi-circle for $x\\in (-2,2],$ and the line segment for $x\\in (2,3].$  So, we can use the additive property of integrals to get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-4}^{-2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-2}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}.\n$$\n\nThe first integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-4,-2].$ The corresponding region is a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =bh \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe second integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (-2,2].$ The corresponding region is a semi-circle of radius $2.$ Thus, we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2}) \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{2}=-2\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe third integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (2,3].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid. Thus, we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2+4)(1) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}-{A}_{2}+{A}_{3} \\\\  & =6-2\\pi +3 \\\\  & =9-2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332684 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332230 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332684 1069 "q-49220" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332684 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332684 1071 "![](images/e0/e0fc07ac8466381b668877e25cd0c9d8d5258a9061548e7b8efb12f75f6a0f04.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. It consists of two lines and the arc of a quarter-circle of radius $2.$ Given $g(t)={\\int }_{-2}^{t}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ determine the value of $g(3).$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332684 1072 #uuid "a213715b-743a-5826-a276-74fb76232453" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332684 1074 "Evaluating $g(t)$ at $t=3,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\ng(3)={\\int }_{-2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral gives the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis shown below.\n\n![](images/92/923b60ed63551bda207f7e3eeb09e62f8b8eb7465a488858bef54bc36ab20baa.png)\n\nNote that the graph above consists of $2$ parts:\n\n- $x\\in [-2,1],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a rectangle. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{1}.$\n\n- $x\\in [1,3],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a quarter-circle. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\ng(3)={\\int }_{-2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-2}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute these integrals:\n\n- To find the first integral where $x\\in [-2,1],$ we compute the area ${A}_{1}$ of the rectangle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =bh \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find the second integral where $x\\in [1,3],$ we compute the area ${A}_{2}$ of the quarter-circle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\  & =\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(3)={\\int }_{-2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=6+\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332685 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332238 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332685 1069 "q-49221" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332685 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332685 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-4}^{-1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332685 1072 #uuid "cac5c79c-f0a3-5733-9707-6baf8522f229" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332685 1074 "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-4}^{-1}{x}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-3+1}}{-3+1}{|}_{-4}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-2}}{(-2)}{|}_{-4}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}{|}_{-4}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{(-1)^{2}}-\\frac{1}{(-4)^{2}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\frac{1}{16}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{15}{32}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332686 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332592 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332686 1069 "q-49223" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332686 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332686 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-2}^{-1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332686 1072 #uuid "08240266-d5ae-5cea-bad2-40e75e833a24" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332686 1074 "Taking the antiderivative and evaluating the difference at the bounds, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{x}^{-5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-5+1}}{-5+1}{|}_{-2}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-4}}{(-4)}{|}_{-2}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4{x}^{4}}{|}_{-2}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{1}{(-1)^{4}}-\\frac{1}{(-2)^{4}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}(1-\\frac{1}{16}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{15}{64}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332687 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332277 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332687 1069 "q-49235" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332687 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332687 1071 "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that ${\\int }_{e}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e+\\frac{ie}{n})\\cdot \\frac{e}{n}].$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332687 1072 #uuid "be4620a0-c65c-5bd5-aaf3-d3285dc13e62" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332687 1074 "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=e.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{e}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{e}{n}=\\frac{b-e}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=2e.\n$$\n\nConsequently,  ${x}_{i}=e+\\frac{ie}{n}$ and $f({x}_{i})=\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{i}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{e}^{2e}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e+\\frac{ie}{n})\\cdot \\frac{e}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ and $b=2e.$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332688 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332221 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332688 1069 "q-49238" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332688 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332688 1071 "The rate, in degrees Farhenheit per month, at which the average midday temperature in New York City varies, does so according to the function $R(t)={t}^{3}+3,$ where $t$ is the number of months since the measurements began. Estimate the total increase in air temperature over the first three months using the trapezoidal rule with $3$ trapezoids of equal height." 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332688 1072 #uuid "2af51dfc-3b4e-5a0b-9ea8-e8839b589130" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332688 1074 "In this context, the area $A$ under the curve $y=R(t)$ over the interval $[0,3]$ represents the total increase in air temperature over the first three months.\n\nThe general formula for the trapezoidal rule states that\n\n$$\nA≈\\frac{1}{2}[R(a)+2(R({t}_{1})+R({t}_{2})+⋯+R({t}_{n-1}))+R(b)]Δt,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of trapezoids and $Δt=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ is the step size.\n\nHere, we have the following values:\n\n- The number of trapezoids is $n=3.$\n\n- The corresponding interval is $[0,3],$ so we have $a=0$ and $b=3.$\n\n- The step size is given by\n\n$$\nΔt=\\frac{b-a}{n}=\\frac{3-0}{3}=1.\n$$\n\nUsing the above values, the general formula becomes\n\n$$\nA≈\\frac{1}{2}[R(0)+2(R(1)+R(2))+R(3)]\\cdot 1.\n$$\n\nTo identify the function values, we now set up a table with the values of $t$ and $R(t):$\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $R(t)$ | $3$ | $4$ | $11$ | $30$ |\n\nSubstituting the appropriate numbers into the above expression gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈\\frac{1}{2}[3+2(4+11)+30]\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{63}{2} \\\\  & =31.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total air temperature increase is approximately ${31.5}^{∘}\\,\\text{F}.$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332689 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332349 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332689 1069 "q-49249" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332689 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332689 1071 "Given that ${\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-5$ and ${\\int }_{4}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=8$ then what is ${\\int }_{0}^{7}(7+3f(x))\\,\\text{d}x?$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332689 1072 #uuid "6d35ab5a-4755-5b7e-ae54-0817b6358371" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332689 1074 "From the adjacent intervals rule, we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{4}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in the known information into the above gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-5)+8={\\int }_{0}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 3={\\int }_{0}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute the desired integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{7}(7+3f(x))\\,\\text{d}x & =7{\\int }_{0}^{7}\\,\\text{d}x+3{\\int }_{0}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =7\\cdot x{|}_{0}^{7}+3(3) \\\\  & =7(7-0)+9 \\\\  & =58.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332690 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332296 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332690 1069 "q-49256" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332690 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332690 1071 "Calculate $\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡3x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332690 1072 #uuid "80f918c1-b02c-51df-b3e3-2c8413bacd10" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332690 1074 "We can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡3x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\operatorname{sec}⁡3x\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe then use the fact that $\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡3x$ to express this as\n\n$$\n\\int {sec}^{2}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=3x.$ Then $\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int {sec}^{2}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {sec}^{2}⁡u\\cdot \\,\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int {sec}^{2}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡3x+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332691 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332411 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332691 1069 "q-49257" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332691 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332691 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{\\pi /8}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332691 1072 #uuid "2e2d21ac-046c-5274-bb89-c9bfaff5e9df" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332691 1074 "We can solve this by substitution. Let $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.$ Then \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.$\n\n| $x$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ | $1$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /8}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}/2}^{1}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{u}{|}_{\\sqrt{2}/2}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{1}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}-1.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332692 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332543 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332692 1069 "q-49258" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332692 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332692 1071 "Calculate $\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{4}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332692 1072 #uuid "df3aa7c1-38d6-5ddf-a732-477c8eceab1e" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332692 1074 "We use the identity $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\