ntity $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332695 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332351 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332695 1069 "q-49277" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332695 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332695 1071 "$\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x{sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332695 1072 #uuid "f3ad6824-baa3-5500-876c-87beb97cf8ef" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332695 1074 "We use the identity $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}$ and rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x{sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Then \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int \\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u}+C \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{-1}+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332696 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332531 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332696 1069 "q-49278" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332696 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332696 1071 "$\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x\\operatorname{sec}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332696 1072 #uuid "8b48a93f-4b08-53d3-bef8-fd2388b47477" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332696 1074 "We use the identity $\\operatorname{sec}⁡3x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x}$ and rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x\\operatorname{sec}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x.$ Then \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x\\,⟹\\,-\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x)^{-1}|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sec}⁡3x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332697 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332449 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332697 1069 "q-49280" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332697 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332697 1071 "Calculate $\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332697 1072 #uuid "73bdd419-9ea0-5251-ace5-0e2f286a0252" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332697 1074 "We can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\operatorname{tan}⁡4