⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{sec}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}-1.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332700 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332266 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332700 1069 "q-49286" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332700 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332700 1071 "![](images/0f/0f8e4dc1dff5673d8fc95375f9632f43bc4942335899e0e6dad11e8693bd6599.png)\n\nA region in the $xy$-plane is bounded by the curve $y=5{sin}^{2}⁡x,$ the $x$-axis and the lines $x=0$ and $x=\\pi .$ The area of the region is" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332700 1072 #uuid "a41d63bd-84eb-5f5c-b954-12d95e773df1" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332700 1074 "The area of the region bounded by the curve $y=5{sin}^{2}⁡x,$ the $x$-axis and the lines $x=0$ and $x=\\pi $ is \n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }5{sin}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet's recall the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${sin}^{2}$ term: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=1-2{sin}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above formula as\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)={sin}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe can now integrate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }5{sin}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{(x-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}([\\pi -\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )]-[0-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot 0)]) \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332701 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332513 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332701 1069 "q-49294" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332701 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332701 1071 "![](images/dc/dcc78643dd44633f778d954de32a955dc5803abf7dc059c30bbbda97bc35413f.png)\n\nA region in the $xy$-plane is bounded by the curve $y=2{cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x,$ the $x$-axis and the lines $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $x=\\pi ,$ as shown above. The area of the region is" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332701 1072 #uuid "489c69ce-d415-5c5f-8b91-c246b0512a1e" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332701 1074 "The area of the region is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(2{cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nRecall  ${cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x=1,$ so we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x & ={cos}^{2}⁡x+({sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we use the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${cos}^{2}⁡x$ term, given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=2{cos}^{2}⁡x-1.\n$$\n\n \n\nRearranging, we have\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+1).\n$$\n\nHence, the area of the region is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi }({cos}^{2}⁡x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi }[\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+1)+1]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+3x)|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\pi +3\\pi )-(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi )-\\frac{3\\pi }{2})] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\pi +\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{9\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332702 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332356 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332702 1069 "q-49299" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332702 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332702 1071 "![](images/20/20844131c3d35b02f0ba7c6567b7765a54f0909e63ee309181dd5a25248dbfd5.png)\n\nA region in the $xy$-plane is bounded by the curve $y=-2{sin}^{2}⁡x+2x,$ the $x$-axis and the lines $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $x=\\pi $, as shown above. The area of the region is" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332702 1072 #uuid "e50b0ecc-a7e1-5f2d-884d-81b7bbf09d3c" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332702 1074 "The area of the region bounded by the curve $y=-2{sin}^{2}⁡x+2x,$ the $x$-axis and the lines $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $x=\\pi $ is \n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(-2{sin}^{2}⁡x+2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet's recall the double-angle formula for cosine that involves the ${sin}^{2}$ term: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=1-2{sin}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above formula as\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-1=-2{sin}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe can now integrate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(-2{sin}^{2}⁡x+2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-1+2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-x+{x}^{2})|}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\pi -\\pi +{\\pi }^{2}]-[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{{2}^{2}}] \\\\  & =-\\pi +{\\pi }^{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}-4\\pi -{\\pi }^{2}+2\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{\\pi }^{2}-2\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi (3\\pi -2)}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332703 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332239 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332703 1069 "q-49307" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332703 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332703 1071 "![](images/ed/edcecf885aaf18a3aa6f6726c0485b1885e37132e73c8964de2576f7da5d61bf.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. It consists of two lines and the arc of a quarter-circle of radius $2.$ Given $g(t)={\\int }_{-3}^{t}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ determine the value of $g(0).$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332703 1072 #uuid "c26693ba-6ca7-5504-80be-c9146f306e3d" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332703 1074 "Evaluating $g(t)$ at $t=0,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\ng(0)={\\int }_{-3}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThis integral gives the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/bc/bc8905f82a9f7525d6c59df337138e9f3b0417a45bc40f4b76fee2ddedac2eab.png)\n\nNote that the graph above consists of $2$ parts:\n\n- $x\\in [-3,-2],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a square. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{1}.$\n\n- $x\\in [-2,0],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a quarter-circle cut out of a rectangle. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\ng(0)={\\int }_{-3}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-3}^{-2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-2}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute these integrals:\n\n- To find the first integral where $x\\in [-3,-2],$ we compute the area ${A}_{1}$ of the square as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & ={s}^{2} \\\\  & ={1}^{2} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find the second integral where $x\\in [-2,0],$ we compute the area ${A}_{2}$ of the quarter-circle cut out of the rectangle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =bh-\\frac{1}{4}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3-\\frac{1}{4}\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\  & =6-\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(0)={\\int }_{-3}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=1+(6-\\pi )=7-\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332704 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332618 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332704 1069 "q-49308" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332704 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332704 1071 "![](images/28/288b3eeaf1168316f2d8f43fa9484032daa8c9006a1c8355b8edfd1186dc2add.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Given $g(t)={\\int }_{-4}^{t}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ determine the value of $g(2).$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332704 1072 #uuid "ba63636c-c207-5bb4-8fb6-5ac0a29fc225" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332704 1074 "Evaluating $g(t)$ at $t=2,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\ng(2)={\\int }_{-4}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThis integral gives the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis shown below.\n\n![](images/9f/9f570d7f04bcf5645a97a6a1d85e72f602c436ef184e841fc4c998c0d5eb8e2e.png)\n\nNote that the graph above consists of $2$ parts:\n\n- $x\\in [-4,-1],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a rectangle. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{1}.$\n\n- $x\\in [-1,2],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a trapezoid. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\ng(2)={\\int }_{-4}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-1}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute these integrals:\n\n- To find the first integral where $x\\in [-4,-1],$ we compute the area ${A}_{1}$ of the rectangle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find the second integral where $x\\in [-1,2],$ we compute the area ${A}_{2}$ of the trapezoid as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot (1+2) \\\\  & =4.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(2) & ={\\int }_{-4}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=6+4.5=10.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332705 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332396 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332705 1069 "q-49309" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332705 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332705 1071 "![](images/af/af84278943fcf2d9c4e3b99c115a4d82672fe50f2bfed8be7be84648985f80df.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. It consists of two lines and the arc of a semi-circle of radius $2.$ Given $g(t)={\\int }_{-3}^{t}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ determine the value of $g(3).$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332705 1072 #uuid "72054613-827c-572f-aba3-072d29c357bd" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332705 1074 "Evaluating $g(t)$ at $t=3,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\ng(3)={\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThis integral gives the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis shown below.\n\n![](images/4d/4d1f408db7fcb99092f7e61afc4c0a25501d3349790e54983f481408c58f9951.png)\n\nNote that the graph above consists of $2$ parts:\n\n- $x\\in [-3,1],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a semi-circle and a rectangle below it. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{1}.$\n\n- $x\\in [1,3],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a trapezoid. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\ng(3)={\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-3}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute these integrals:\n\n- To find the first integral where $x\\in [-3,1],$ we compute the area ${A}_{1}$ of the semi-circle and the rectangle below it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2}+bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2}+4\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2\\pi +4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find the second integral where $x\\in [1,3],$ we compute the area ${A}_{2}$ of the trapezoid as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}h(a+b) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot (1+3) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(3) & ={\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=(2\\pi +4)+4=2\\pi +8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332706 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332344 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332706 1069 "q-49315" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332706 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332706 1071 "A bicycle tire has a pressure of $6\\,\\text{psi}.$ It is being inflated, and the pressure is increasing at a rate of $4t\\,\\text{psi}$ per second, where $t$ is a time, in seconds, since the start of the inflation. The tire has a slow puncture that reduces pressure at a rate of $(t+2)\\,\\text{psi}$ per second. Identify an expression for $P(t),$ the total pressure in the tire at any time $t\\ge 0.$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332706 1072 #uuid "7f052148-475f-5e9d-b934-2204e76a0ded" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332706 1074 "Let $p(t)=4t-(t+2)=3t-2$ be the rate at which the pressure in the tire is changing. Note that we subtract $(t+2)$ to indicate the rate at which the pressure is decreasing.\n\nLet $P(T)$ be the pressure in the tire after $T$ seconds. Then, using our formula for the accumulation of change, we have\n\n$$\nP(T)=P(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}p(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known information and carrying out the necessary integration gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(T) & =P(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}p(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6+{\\int }_{0}^{T}(3t-2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6+{(\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}-2t)|}_{0}^{T} \\\\  & =6+\\frac{3}{2}{T}^{2}-2T \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{T}^{2}-2T+6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's rewrite our final answer in terms of the original variable $t.$ We have\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}-2t+6.\n$$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332707 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332397 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332707 1069 "q-49325" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332707 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332707 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{3(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-1)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332707 1072 #uuid "22c49d6a-442c-592d-b81d-9330777b0cac" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332707 1074 "We are using the identity\n\n$$\n{tan}^{2}⁡x+1={sec}^{2}⁡x\\,⟹\\,{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x-1.\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{3(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-1)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =3{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{({sec}^{2}⁡x-1)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\,{|}_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})-3\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2-3\\cdot \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =6-2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332708 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332576 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332708 1069 "q-49332" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332708 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332708 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi /3}\\frac{1-{cot}^{4}⁡x}{{cot}^{2}⁡x-1}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332708 1072 #uuid "d70cb193-c0f3-511e-a986-3ca7b8cef3f9" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332708 1074 "In this case, we have to use the identity  \n\n$$\n1+{cot}^{2}⁡x={csc}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi /3}\\frac{1-{cot}^{4}⁡x}{{cot}^{2}⁡x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi /3}\\frac{(1-{cot}^{2}⁡x)(1+{cot}^{2}⁡x)}{{cot}^{2}⁡x-1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi /3}(1+{cot}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi /3}{csc}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡x{|}_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi /3} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\frac{2\\pi }{3}-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}-0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332709 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332318 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332709 1069 "q-49353" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332709 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332709 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{2x-3}{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332709 1072 #uuid "06189516-15f6-57fc-9a99-e7cebf7baa9b" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332709 1074 "The denominator of the integrand doesn't divide the numerator exactly, so we need to decompose it into its quotient and remainder. For this, we can use synthetic division.\n\nWe start by drawing up our synthetic division table:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & x & {x}^{0} \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3 \\\\  & ↓ & 2 \\\\  & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo from the synthetic division, we get that\n\n$$\n\\frac{2x-3}{x-1}=2-\\frac{1}{x-1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{2x-3}{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}(2-\\frac{1}{x-1})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}2\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2x{|}_{2}^{3}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|{|}_{2}^{3} \\\\  & =2(3-2)-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3-1|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2-1|) \\\\  & =2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332710 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332565 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332710 1069 "q-49354" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332710 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332710 1071 "$\\int \\frac{5x+7}{x+2}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332710 1072 #uuid "ec1f8b08-73c8-5230-ba02-f32931218a96" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332710 1074 "The denominator of the integrand doesn't divide the numerator exactly, so we need to decompose it into its quotient and remainder. For this, we can use synthetic division.\n\nWe start by drawing up our synthetic division table:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & x & {x}^{0} \\\\ -2 & 5 & 7 \\\\  & ↓ & -10 \\\\  & 5 & -3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo from the synthetic division, we get that\n\n$$\n\\frac{5x+7}{x+2}=5-\\frac{3}{x+2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5x+7}{x+5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (5-\\frac{3}{x+2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5\\int \\text{d}x-3\\int \\frac{1}{x+2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5x-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332711 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332353 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332711 1069 "q-49357" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332711 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332711 1071 "Calculate $\\int \\frac{x+1}{4x+1}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332711 1072 #uuid "7806e71e-9cb7-55be-836c-04e6fc97f767" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332711 1074 "First, we pull out the factor of $4$ from the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x+1}{4x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{x+1}{4(x+\\frac{1}{4})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{x+1}{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we draw up our synthetic division table to decompose the integrand into its quotient and remainder.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & x & {x}^{0} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{4} & 1 & 1 \\\\  & ↓ & -\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & 1 & \\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo from the synthetic division, we get that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+1}{(x+\\frac{1}{4})} & =1+\\frac{(\\frac{3}{4})}{(x+\\frac{1}{4})} \\\\  & =1+\\frac{3}{4(x+\\frac{1}{4})} \\\\  & =1+\\frac{3}{4x+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x+1}{4x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int (1+\\frac{3}{4x+1})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\int \\text{d}x+3\\int \\frac{1}{4x+1}\\,\\text{d}x] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[x+\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|4x+1|]+C \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{4}+\\frac{3}{16}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|4x+1|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332712 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332583 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332712 1069 "q-49360" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332712 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332712 1071 "Calculate $\\int (\\frac{1}{1+x}+\\frac{1}{1-x})\\,\\text{d}x.$ Assume that $K$ is an arbitrary positive constant." 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332712 1072 #uuid "1dc5a85a-4afe-5495-af86-22c90f6982a7" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332712 1074 "Let's solve this using the general formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\frac{1}{1+x}+\\frac{1}{1-x})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{1+x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{1-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1+x|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1-x|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{1+x}{1-x}|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡K \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|\\frac{1+x}{1-x}|)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the laws of logarithms to write the result as a single logarithm." 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332713 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332627 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332713 1069 "q-49386" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332713 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332713 1071 "Given that $u=x-1$, which integral is equivalent to $\\int x\\sqrt[5]{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x?$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332713 1072 #uuid "d725487a-5806-5f7e-95bc-a9839bf05cf1" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332713 1074 "Since $u=x-1,$ then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe also note that\n$x=u+1.$\n\nNow we can rewrite the integral in terms of $u:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\sqrt[5]{x-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (u+1)(\\sqrt[5]{u})(\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\int (u+1)({u}^{1/5})(\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\int ({u}^{6/5}+{u}^{1/5})\\text{d}u\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332714 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332535 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332714 1069 "q-49387" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332714 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332714 1071 "Given that $u=1+{x}^{2}$, which integral is equivalent to $\\int \\frac{{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}?$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332714 1072 #uuid "e0542bde-b2c9-57c4-9ab2-47066e6c42b4" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332714 1074 "Since $u=1+{x}^{2}$, then we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x\\,⟹\\,x\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe also note that\n${x}^{2}=u-1$\n\nNow we can rewrite the integral in terms of $u:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}} & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}\\cdot {x}^{2}\\cdot x\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot (u-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int ({u}^{1/2}-{u}^{-1/2})\\text{d}u\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332715 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332641 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332715 1069 "q-49393" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332715 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332715 1071 "$\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{(3-5s)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}s=$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332715 1072 #uuid "44b08892-d784-5133-8162-d9d2c70e3c9d" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332715 1074 "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int (3-5s)^{-2/3}\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nNow, we let  $u=3-5s.$ Then differentiating with respect to $s$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}s}=-5\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}s=-\\frac{1}{5}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $u$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (3-5s)^{-2/3}\\,\\text{d}s & =\\int {u}^{-2/3}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{5}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\int {u}^{-2/3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{1/3}{u}^{1/3}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{5}{u}^{1/3}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{5}(3-5s)^{1/3}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{5}\\sqrt[3]{3-5s}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}[-\\frac{3}{5}\\sqrt[3]{3-5s}+C] & =-\\frac{3}{5}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}[\\sqrt[3]{3-5s}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(C) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{5}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}[(3-5s)^{1/3}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(C) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(3-5s)^{-2/3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(3-5s)+0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}(3-5s)^{-2/3}\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =(3-5s)^{-2/3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{(3-5s)^{2}}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332716 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332608 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332716 1069 "q-49398" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332716 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332716 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-2}^{0}\\frac{x+4}{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332716 1072 #uuid "72f64856-0d6e-5756-9e66-30ca70000360" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332716 1074 "The denominator of the integrand doesn't divide the numerator exactly, so we need to decompose it into its quotient and remainder. For this, we can use synthetic division.\n\nWe start by drawing up our synthetic division table:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & x & {x}^{0} \\\\ -3 & 1 & 4 \\\\  & ↓ & -3 \\\\  & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo from the synthetic division, we get that\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+4}{x+3}=1+\\frac{1}{x+3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{0}\\frac{x+4}{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}(1+\\frac{1}{x+3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{-2}^{0}\\frac{1}{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{|}_{-2}^{0}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+3|{|}_{-2}^{0} \\\\  & =(0-(-2))+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|0+3|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|-2+3|) \\\\  & =2+\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332717 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332561 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332717 1069 "q-49404" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332717 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332717 1071 "$\\int \\frac{x+3}{x-4}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332717 1072 #uuid "ae52f11d-c818-5b04-9e5a-10577e3d8339" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332717 1074 "The denominator of the integrand doesn't divide the numerator exactly, so we need to decompose it into its quotient and remainder. For this, we can use synthetic division.\n\nWe start by drawing up our synthetic division table:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & x & {x}^{0} \\\\ 4 & 1 & 3 \\\\  & ↓ & 4 \\\\  & 1 & 7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo from the synthetic division, we get that\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+3}{x-4}=1+\\frac{7}{x-4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x+3}{x-4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (1+\\frac{7}{x-4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\text{d}x+7\\int \\frac{1}{x-4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x+7\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332718 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332319 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332718 1069 "q-49411" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332718 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332718 1071 "Calculate $\\int \\frac{3x-2}{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332718 1072 #uuid "77cb94ec-923d-5f13-9587-b73868bfbd09" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332718 1074 "The denominator of the integrand doesn't divide the numerator exactly, so we need to decompose it into its quotient and remainder. For this, we can use synthetic division.\n\nWe start by drawing up our synthetic division table:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & x & {x}^{0} \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2 \\\\  & ↓ & -3 \\\\  & 3 & -5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo from the synthetic division, we get that\n\n$$\n\\frac{3x-2}{x+1}=3-\\frac{5}{x+1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3x-2}{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (3-\\frac{5}{x+1})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int \\text{d}x-5\\int \\frac{1}{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3x-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332719 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332586 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332719 1069 "q-49415" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332719 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332719 1071 "$\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+5x+1}{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332719 1072 #uuid "8facc4fe-78b5-55cc-b01a-0ed033eb2116" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332719 1074 "We first need to decompose the integrand into its quotient and remainder parts, which we can do using synthetic division.\n\nWe draw up a synthetic division table:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ -3 & 1 & 5 & 1 \\\\  & ↓ & -3 & -6 \\\\  & 1 & 2 & -5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo from the synthetic division, we get that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}+5x+1}{x+3}=x+2-\\frac{5}{x+3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}+5x+1}{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (x+2-\\frac{5}{x+3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int x\\,\\text{d}x+2\\int \\text{d}x-5\\int \\frac{1}{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+2x-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+3|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332720 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332422 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332720 1069 "q-49421" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332720 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332720 1071 "$\\int \\frac{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}-x+2}{x+2}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332720 1072 #uuid "f73bd1c4-b9fa-52db-a8ab-fa70bf82958b" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332720 1074 "We first need to decompose the integrand into its quotient and remainder parts, which we can do using synthetic division.\n\nWe draw up a synthetic division table:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ -2 & 1 & -3 & -1 & 2 \\\\  & ↓ & -2 & 10 & -18 \\\\  & 1 & -5 & 9 & -16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo from the synthetic division, we get that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}-x+2}{x+2}={x}^{2}-5x+9-\\frac{16}{x+2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}-x+2}{x+2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int ({x}^{2}-5x+9-\\frac{16}{x+2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-5\\int x\\,\\text{d}x+9\\int \\text{d}x-16\\int \\frac{1}{x+2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}-\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{2}+9x-16\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+2|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332721 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332252 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332721 1069 "q-49437" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332721 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332721 1071 "Calculate the integral $\\int \\sqrt[3]{(y+11)^{5}}\\,\\text{d}y.$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332721 1072 #uuid "dab90c5a-327c-5636-bfaa-e6da096f9912" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332721 1074 "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int (y+11)^{5/3}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nNow, we let $u=y+11.$ Then differentiating with respect to $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}y}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}y=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe now write our integral in terms of $u,$ calculate the integral as normal, and write the final answer in terms of $u$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (y+11)^{5/3}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int {u}^{5/3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{{u}^{8/3}}{8/3}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}(y+11)^{8/3}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}\\sqrt[3]{(y+11)^{8}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}[\\frac{3}{8}\\sqrt[3]{(y+11)^{8}}] & =\\frac{3}{8}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}[\\sqrt[3]{(y+11)^{8}}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}[(y+11)^{8/3}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}\\cdot \\frac{8}{3}(y+11)^{5/3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(y+11)+0 \\\\  & =(y+11)^{5/3}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{(y+11)^{5}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332722 1068 #edb/ref 17592186332486 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332722 1069 "q-49446" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332722 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332722 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{5\\pi /6}\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x-1}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533424 17592186045585 true] [17592186332722 1072 #uuid "71ebda71-dbd6-53ac-9e9c-22ae8c3a8413" 